sara_support: FYI escalated from Acme - their toolcall keeps 400'ing. They say error is \"Invalid tool_args JSON: Unexpected token ',' at position 24\". Repro attached.

sara_support: customer snippet (React client):
```js
const body = JSON.stringify({
  tool_name: 'geo_resolve',
  // oops: double-stringify the nested object
  tool_args: JSON.stringify({ lat: 37.7749, lon: -122.4194, }),
});
await fetch('/v1/call-tool', {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
  body,
});
```

sara_support: The important bit: inner object ends up as `{"lat":37.7749,"lon":-122.4194,}` (note trailing comma) so the server receives JSON with a string for tool_args that is itself invalid JSON.

mike_dev: Can you paste the exact request body the server saw (from logs) and the response?

sara_support: From logs (redacted):
```
REQUEST BODY: {"tool_name":"geo_resolve","tool_args":"{\"lat\":37.7749,\"lon\":-122.4194,}"}
RESPONSE 400: {"error":"Invalid \"tool_args\" JSON: Unexpected token ',' at position 27"}
```

julia_engineer: Yep that lines up. Two issues: 1) client double-JSON.stringify -> nested JSON string, 2) trailing comma in inner object (some transpilers/formatters preserve it). Many JS engines allow trailing commas in object literals, but JSON.parse does not.

julia_engineer: Quick fixes for customer: remove JSON.stringify on the nested object so tool_args is an object (not a string), or if they must send nested serialized JSON, ensure you strip trailing commas before serializing. Example fix:
```js
// preferred - send object directly
const body = {
  tool_name: 'geo_resolve',
  tool_args: { lat: 37.7749, lon: -122.4194 },
};
fetch('/v1/call-tool', { headers: { 'Content-Type':'application/json' }, method: 'POST', body: JSON.stringify(body) });
```

julia_engineer: If they can't change the client quickly, server-side workaround: detect when tool_args is a string and try a lenient parse that strips trailing commas before JSON.parse, but that should be temporary.

mike_dev: Agreed. Can we also improve the error message to say 'tool_args must be a valid JSON object or a JSON-serialized object string (no trailing commas)?' Right now the parse error is cryptic.

infra-bot: Filed issue #INF-842: Add a diagnostic that checks for double-stringified tool_args and returns a clearer helper with example payloads. Also adding a one-off safety parse with warning in logs for the next 48h.

sara_support: I'll send the suggested body patch to the customer and share the INF-842 ticket. They were using a legacy wrapper that does JSON.stringify on every value.

mike_dev: Thanks — once customer confirms fixed, mark as resolved. Also ping @julia_engineer when INF-842 lands so we can roll the warning config back. :thumbsup:

sara_support: Will do.
